\section{What the columns report}{
The columns \code{profile_loglik}, \code{profile_penalty} and \code{profile_pen_loglik}
belong to the INNER criterion, the penalized log-likelihood of the
coefficients at the hyperparameters in force. At the point where the
coefficients are held at the values of the row, \code{profile_loglik} is the
log-likelihood without the penalty, \code{profile_penalty} is the penalty with
the sign that makes the two add up, that is the logarithm of the prior
density of the coefficients, \eqn{-\tfrac12 P_\lambda(\beta)} with its
normalizing constant, and \code{profile_pen_loglik} is their sum, which is the
quantity that the refit maximizes. The constant depends on the
hyperparameters and not on the coefficients, so the penalty is not
necessarily negative, and with the hyperparameters held its changes along
the grid are those of the quadratic form. A model without penalty has
\code{profile_penalty} equal to zero, and the two log-likelihoods then
coincide.

The column \code{outer_criterion} belongs to the OUTER criterion (\code{\link[=reml]{reml()}},
\code{\link[=ml]{ml()}}, \code{\link[=aic]{aic()}} or \code{\link[=bic]{bic()}}), a different function of a different object:
the value, on the sign and the scale that the fit reports in
\code{fit@criterion}, of the criterion that chooses the hyperparameters or the
coefficients that the criterion estimates. It is not the profile of that
criterion over the held coefficients in any sense that the first three
columns have for the inner one, and it is \code{NA} when the point involved no
outer search: when the fit has no outer criterion, or when the
hyperparameters are held (\code{hyper = "held"}) and the criterion estimates no
coefficient.

The column \code{converged} states that the point is at the maximum of its
refit. It is \code{TRUE} when the stopping rule of the refit fired, or when the
Newton decrement at the point is below the tolerance of
\code{\link[=statmod_certificate]{statmod_certificate()}}, and in both cases the final inner fit converged.
The second condition is needed because the line search of the outer
optimizer can exhaust its backtracks at a point that is already the
optimum, without recording that its stopping rule fired. The row of the
attribute \code{maximum} reports instead \code{fit@converged}, which is only the
flag of the stopping rule. A refit that signals an error gives a row of
\code{NA} with \code{converged = FALSE}, and the function warns.
