\section{The coefficients to hold: \code{which}}{
A label has the form \code{"parameter:coefficient"}: the distribution
parameter whose equation carries the coefficient, a colon, and the name of
the coefficient as the second column of the table of \code{\link[=coef_labels]{coef_labels()}} gives
it, which is the name that \code{coef()} and \code{summary()} print. The labels of a
fit are the row names of \code{summary(fit)}, for example \code{"mu:(Intercept)"},
\code{"mu:x"} or \code{"sigma:s(z).1"}. Labels must be unique.

The coefficient of the effect of a covariate \code{z} on the parameter \code{theta}
of a \code{\link[modelterms7:nl]{modelterms7::nl()}} term, written \code{nl(~ a * exp(-theta * x), theta ~ 1 + z)} in the equation of \code{mu}, has the label
\code{"mu:nl.theta.z"}. The name after the colon is \code{nl.}, the name of the
parameter of the term, a dot and the name of the coefficient of its
subformula, so that the intercept of \code{theta} is
\code{"mu:nl.theta.(Intercept)"}. A parameter of the term without a subformula
is a coefficient by itself, as in \code{"mu:nl.a"}.

The position of a break-point of \code{\link[modelterms7:seg]{modelterms7::seg()}} is the coefficient
\code{psi1} (then \code{psi2}, and so on) of the term, as in \code{"mu:seg.psi1"}. When
the position has a subformula, as in \code{seg(w, psi ~ x)}, its coefficients
are \code{"mu:seg.psi1.(Intercept)"} and \code{"mu:seg.psi1.x"}. The names of all
the coefficients of a fit are the row names of
\code{statmodels7:::coef_labels(fit@spec, statmodels7:::statmod_design(fit@spec))}.

When two or more positions of the same \code{seg()} term without subformulas
are in \code{which}, the positions of a model are ordered and distinct: the
combinations of the grid in which a position is not larger than the
position before it are not evaluated and are not in the table.
}

\section{The scale}{
The values are on the scale of the coefficient in the stacked vector of the
fit, which is unconstrained: a coefficient of a linear predictor is on
the scale of the link, and so are the coefficients of the predictor of a
parameter of a nonlinear term, the positions of the break-points and the
parameters of a covariance structure. Values on another scale must be
mapped to it by the caller. A monotone map relabels the grid and leaves the
columns of the profile unchanged.
}

\section{The hyperparameters, and the coefficients of the criterion}{
With \code{hyper = "held"} the hyperparameters (the smoothing parameters and the
scales of the random effects) stay at the values of the fit, and with
\code{hyper = "refit"} the outer search is run again at every point. In both
cases, when the outer criterion estimates coefficients (\code{\link[=reml]{reml()}} and
\code{\link[=ml]{ml()}} estimate the unpenalized coefficients of every parameter except the
first), those coefficients are estimated by the criterion at every point
and are not carried to the joint mode of the penalized likelihood, which
\code{\link[=statmod_restrict]{statmod_restrict()}} does. A held coefficient that the criterion would
have estimated is held, and the criterion estimates the rest. The cost of
a point is then that of an outer search over those coordinates, and it
exceeds the cost of a restricted fit.

The outer optimizer is the default one of \code{\link[=statmod]{statmod()}}, and the stopping
rules, the inner method and the information are those of the fit.
}

\section{Coefficients that cannot be held yet}{
A coefficient under a penalty with a kink (a lasso, a SCAD or an MCP), a
coefficient of a model with a structural term (\code{\link[modelterms7:gas]{modelterms7::gas()}},
\code{\link[modelterms7:regime]{modelterms7::regime()}}, the marginal break-point term) and any
coefficient with \code{hyper = "refit"} in a model whose kinked penalties are
tuned by a path signal an error. An aliased coefficient has no estimate to
profile and signals an error.
}

